	task.5AFFF9A2B85E4222.title: "Reactor core placed"
	task.5B4F701BDA2DB16A.title: "Reactor room shielded"
	task.5CB33CAAAB4050F8.title: "Molten Salt Reactor core placed"
	task.5DD6075E35E06CC0.title: "I found the readout"
	task.6056729B0B53CE5B.title: "Brief accepted"
	task.611D533430A52B14.title: "A Tier I Factory is running"
	task.6619F95B6270A25F.title: "Mechanical Press running"
	task.67DF6CC29357B28D.title: "Quantum Link online"
	task.6AA5ED0DEC2DA00F.title: "A Sim block is producing"
	task.6CFC940FBD59FD94.title: "ME network is receiving power"
	task.6D6E7208AE195824.title: "Advanced Switch placed and powered"
	task.6F060F1FDB0B7728.title: "Control Rod Module bound"
	task.6FE0643938CCE5D8.title: "Logistics network built and bound"
	task.738C4BB0994D3654.title: "I sold my wood"
	task.7558934886A284CB.title: "Reactor running clean"
	task.765F224ED5DE63C5.title: "Fuel Reprocessor running"
	task.77B46865CD772429.title: "Metallurgic Infuser running"
	task.7849BA22BADFFAD9.title: "I switched algorithms"
	task.789D4DD07BF93807.title: "I'm ready for more"
	task.7930ED5ED49F7F36.title: "Chemical Injection Chamber running"
	task.79332B596820F51A.title: "Reactor reacted to network loss"
	task.7B25D9C3E081F46A.title: "I sold a component back"
	task.7F75E2CE1DB03326.title: "My Rack is running"
	task.7FE2CE15C2E4D28E.title: "Core Switch placed and powered"
	chapter.36D67E8375B9B07B.title: "Operations Audit"
	quest.0311053CC308236A.title: "Contract - Second Life Workshop"
	quest.0311053CC308236A.quest_desc: ["Your parts wear out under heat and die in crashes. They no longer vanish - they go DEFECTIVE and stay in their slot.", "Buy a Salvage Bench: it strips dead parts for metals or refurbishes them with Thermal Paste."]
	task.39F0B058FA0D9A0B.title: "A component went defective"
	quest.27A1F839EC576A08.title: "Incident Report - First Casualty"
	quest.27A1F839EC576A08.quest_desc: ["A component in one of your machines has reached the end of its life and gone defective.", "Find it: the bay it sat in has stopped, and the part's tooltip says so in red. Pull it at the Workbench."]
	task.188667307F5C1EA0.title: "Salvaged a component"
	quest.53BB220E2F95535A.title: "Job - Stripped for Parts"
	quest.53BB220E2F95535A.quest_desc: ["Put a component on the Salvage Bench's mat and run Salvage. Gold, copper, redstone, quartz, iron - a flagship gives more than a budget card, dead or alive.", "Nothing salvaged can be sold to the Shop. It feeds your building, not your wallet."]
	task.100728E5C1182261.title: "Refurbished a component"
	quest.003E46AA52E9B81B.title: "Job - Back From the Dead"
	quest.003E46AA52E9B81B.quest_desc: ["Refurbish a damaged or defective part: one Thermal Paste, ten seconds, and it is back at 75%. Then 60%, then 45% - three lives, then salvage only.", "The Shop pays half for a refurbished part. It knows."]
	quest.4D0FB24BB828DF06.title: "Contract - The Auditor Arrives"
	quest.4D0FB24BB828DF06.quest_desc: ["A Certification Terminal hangs on a wall inside a sealed room and audits it every five seconds: sixteen criteria, four per tier.", "Hosting Racks in a certified room earn more. Buy one and hang it where your racks are."]
	quest.420C05A391210A1C.title: "Contract - Battery Cabinet"
	quest.420C05A391210A1C.quest_desc: ["A UPS sits between your feed and your racks: 4 million FE, 2,000 FE/t in and out, feeding every machine it touches.", "Tier III wants one at half charge; Tier IV wants two."]
	task.087337E9C83BF8D4.title: "A UPS reached 50% charge"
	quest.3F997FCFD4B45DF9.title: "Acceptance Test - Batteries Full"
	quest.3F997FCFD4B45DF9.quest_desc: ["Feed a UPS until its front bar shows at least half. Right-click it for the exact figure."]
	task.0A5B51B80B9F9E5B.title: "A room reached Tier I"
	quest.554D53F22D8AD91F.title: "Certification - Tier I, Basic"
	quest.554D53F22D8AD91F.quest_desc: ["Sealed room, a running machine, no blocked intake or exhaust, a cooling unit, nothing throttling.", "The plaque lights its first lamp. Hosting here pays +5%."]
	task.39C724C486D80770.title: "A room reached Tier II"
	quest.79EEAF55ABEBA252.title: "Certification - Tier II, Redundant"
	quest.79EEAF55ABEBA252.quest_desc: ["N+1: every running server keeps full power with its largest PSU removed, two cooling units, a dust filter on every machine, no defective part in the room.", "Hosting here pays +15%."]
	task.58913C49AB634934.title: "A room reached Tier III"
	quest.3D8C6EC6791C53E2.title: "Certification - Tier III, Maintainable"
	quest.3D8C6EC6791C53E2.quest_desc: ["A UPS at half charge, room air at or below 27°C, and the room entered only through Server Doors.", "Hosting here pays +30%."]
	task.49EBA2947A61B83F.title: "A room reached Tier IV"
	quest.729DA942F9F26B7B.title: "Certification - Tier IV, Fault Tolerant"
	quest.729DA942F9F26B7B.quest_desc: ["2N: every server runs on any one of its PSUs alone, two air conditioners, two charged UPS, and the Tier III conditions held for ten unbroken minutes.", "One throttling rack resets the clock. Hosting here pays +50%."]
	task.59DCAC50293F639A.title: "Reached Small Business customers"
	quest.5A4C9386E19A5138.title: "Reputation - Small Business"
	quest.5A4C9386E19A5138.quest_desc: ["Every bay-tick of hosting you deliver builds reputation; throttling, dark customer servers and crashes tear it down.", "At 150 points small businesses start signing: +8% on hosting income."]
	task.05A4723F749CF5FF.title: "Reached Mid-Market customers"
	quest.39B75A1540A30A88.title: "Reputation - Mid-Market"
	quest.39B75A1540A30A88.quest_desc: ["350 points, and mid-market companies come calling: +18%.", "They only host in a Tier I room. Reputation opens the door; certification decides where it leads."]
	task.04C9A9101F361FA1.title: "Reached Enterprise customers"
	quest.1EE0D25FFFD6F26F.title: "Reputation - Enterprise"
	quest.1EE0D25FFFD6F26F.quest_desc: ["600 points: enterprise customers, +30%. They want a Tier II room."]
	task.63C845B392510F89.title: "Reached Finance \\& Government customers"
	quest.2F19D88640C99F8B.title: "Reputation - Finance \\& Government"
	quest.2F19D88640C99F8B.quest_desc: ["850 points: banks and ministries, +45%. Nothing below Tier III will do for them.", "A single afternoon of crashes can undo this. Keeping it is the job."]
	chapter.107F801D40DDC457.title: "Hall of Fame"
	quest.7ED72F7464A21001.title: "Hall of Fame - Deep Blue"
	quest.7ED72F7464A21001.quest_desc: ["11.4 GFLOPS. In May 1997 this IBM machine beat Garry Kasparov over six games - the first time a computer took a match from a reigning world champion.", "Your data center now out-computes it. Any single CPU does. It is the first rung."]
	task.702CEBDBE67C5272.title: "Out-computed Deep Blue"
	quest.2DF49C497D34AA06.title: "Hall of Fame - ASCI Red"
	quest.2DF49C497D34AA06.quest_desc: ["1.3 TFLOPS. Sandia's ASCI Red was the first computer to break a teraflop, in 1997, built to simulate nuclear weapons after the test ban.", "Nine thousand Pentium Pro chips in a room the size of a tennis court. You did it with a rack."]
	task.088A9F54F68915BF.title: "Out-computed ASCI Red"
	quest.62CA4CB1DBA43706.title: "Hall of Fame - Earth Simulator"
	quest.62CA4CB1DBA43706.quest_desc: ["35.9 TFLOPS. Japan's Earth Simulator topped the TOP500 from 2002 to 2004, modelling the whole planet's climate - and shocked the US into a supercomputing race."]
	task.166A8130BAE72D49.title: "Out-computed Earth Simulator"
	quest.2DD244B6E3152A08.title: "Hall of Fame - Blue Gene/L"
	quest.2DD244B6E3152A08.quest_desc: ["280.6 TFLOPS. Lawrence Livermore's Blue Gene/L held the top spot from 2004 to 2008 with 131,072 small, cool, low-clocked cores - the first machine to win by being efficient rather than fast."]
	task.2309BD2541DE274C.title: "Out-computed Blue Gene/L"
	quest.77DB7FB2FD66C22C.title: "Hall of Fame - Roadrunner"
	quest.77DB7FB2FD66C22C.quest_desc: ["1.03 PFLOPS. Los Alamos' Roadrunner was the first petaflop machine, in June 2008 - six months before your calendar starts. It ran on PlayStation 3 processors.", "The first petaflop. Welcome to the big league."]
	task.7CBF2988FAF08A01.title: "Out-computed Roadrunner"
	quest.11A3D01E36178DBC.title: "Hall of Fame - Jaguar"
	quest.11A3D01E36178DBC.quest_desc: ["1.76 PFLOPS. Oak Ridge's Jaguar, TOP500 #1 in November 2009: a quarter million AMD Opteron cores. Your Threadwhip server chips are its direct descendants."]
	task.7CACF3A0321F37B6.title: "Out-computed Jaguar"
	quest.6CB4EAF0250838F6.title: "Hall of Fame - Tianhe-1A"
	quest.6CB4EAF0250838F6.quest_desc: ["2.57 PFLOPS. China's Tianhe-1A took #1 in November 2010 - the first top machine to get most of its speed from graphics cards. Seven thousand of them.", "The GPU era begins. You are living in it."]
	task.5F2466F161C71BC0.title: "Out-computed Tianhe-1A"
	quest.68DED586D76D9BEE.title: "Hall of Fame - K computer"
	quest.68DED586D76D9BEE.quest_desc: ["10.5 PFLOPS. RIKEN's K computer was the first past ten petaflops, in 2011, on 88,000 SPARC processors - and it did it without a single GPU."]
	task.0AB1FEDA405FD7A8.title: "Out-computed K computer"
	quest.03788F03EEEBB409.title: "Hall of Fame - Titan"
	quest.03788F03EEEBB409.quest_desc: ["17.6 PFLOPS. Oak Ridge's Titan, #1 in November 2012, was Jaguar rebuilt with 18,688 Nvidia Tesla K20X cards - the machine that made GPU computing respectable."]
	task.2CAA378E20570A70.title: "Out-computed Titan"
	quest.163686A85A1EEB24.title: "Hall of Fame - Tianhe-2"
	quest.163686A85A1EEB24.quest_desc: ["33.9 PFLOPS. Tianhe-2 held #1 for three years from June 2013, on three million Intel Xeon and Xeon Phi cores. Forty megawatts, cooling included."]
	task.74EAA9E9CCE4177A.title: "Out-computed Tianhe-2"
	quest.5A60E6CB82C8DE84.title: "Hall of Fame - Sunway TaihuLight"
	quest.5A60E6CB82C8DE84.quest_desc: ["93 PFLOPS. TaihuLight, #1 from June 2016, ran entirely on Chinese-designed processors - ten million cores, no Intel, no Nvidia, no imports."]
	task.337EFEE8BE6F5754.title: "Out-computed Sunway TaihuLight"
	quest.7F2CEFE3E592DC49.title: "Hall of Fame - Summit"
	quest.7F2CEFE3E592DC49.quest_desc: ["148.6 PFLOPS. Oak Ridge's Summit, #1 from June 2018: 27,648 Nvidia V100s on IBM POWER9 hosts, water-cooled, the machine most of the first big AI models were trained next to.", "From here on at least half of the Compute must come from datacenter-grade hardware - Tensor or Impulse accelerators, or server CPUs. No hyperscaler runs on gaming cards."]
	task.2A8FCC3B15271CDD.title: "Out-computed Summit"
	quest.302116C5B5CB4C0E.title: "Hall of Fame - Fugaku"
	quest.302116C5B5CB4C0E.quest_desc: ["442 PFLOPS. RIKEN's Fugaku, #1 from June 2020: 158,976 ARM processors, no GPUs, and the machine that modelled how the pandemic spread through a train carriage.", "From here on at least half of the Compute must come from datacenter-grade hardware - Tensor or Impulse accelerators, or server CPUs. No hyperscaler runs on gaming cards."]
	task.5A7E681DBCCFF0A0.title: "Out-computed Fugaku"
	quest.018AFAF1B1058463.title: "Hall of Fame - Frontier"
	quest.018AFAF1B1058463.quest_desc: ["1.1 EFLOPS. Oak Ridge's Frontier, May 2022: the first exaflop machine ever measured. 9,472 nodes, 37,888 AMD MI250X GPUs, 21 megawatts.", "Exascale. You built a data center that out-computes the fastest machine in history.", "From here on at least half of the Compute must come from datacenter-grade hardware - Tensor or Impulse accelerators, or server CPUs. No hyperscaler runs on gaming cards."]
	task.1EA78380CFD38C97.title: "Out-computed Frontier"
	quest.7931E61A4FA4E932.title: "Hall of Fame - More Compute Than Googol"
	quest.7931E61A4FA4E932.quest_desc: ["~9 EFLOPS. In May 2022 Googol announced eight TPU v4 pods in one Oklahoma campus, about nine exaflops of machine-learning compute - the largest publicly claimed by any single company.", "Past the end of history - but not past 2026. Two campuses still stand above you.", "From here on at least half of the Compute must come from datacenter-grade hardware - Tensor or Impulse accelerators, or server CPUs. No hyperscaler runs on gaming cards."]
	task.0A81457782C134F5.title: "Out-computed Googol"
	chapter.09362A2147DF169E.title: "Mining Records"
	quest.539F0CAADF2559E8.title: "Mining Records - Two Machines"
	quest.539F0CAADF2559E8.quest_desc: ["7 MH/s. On 12 January 2009 Satoshi Nakamoto sent Hal Finney ten bitcoin - the first transaction. The whole network was their two computers.", "Any CPU you own out-mines it. Every miner since has stood on this rung."]
	task.1092AA3EC1AFAC02.title: "Out-hashed Two Machines"
	quest.7890386AAA0D0CA0.title: "Mining Records - Pizza Day"
	quest.7890386AAA0D0CA0.quest_desc: ["28 MH/s. On 22 May 2010 Laszlo Hanyecz paid 10,000 BTC for two pizzas - the first thing bitcoin ever bought. The network that priced them was smaller than one of your GPUs."]
	task.14D2B66C7C584586.title: "Out-hashed Pizza Day"
	quest.71504F863888912A.title: "Mining Records - The First GPU Miners"
	quest.71504F863888912A.quest_desc: ["320 MH/s. In July 2010 the first GPU mining code appeared and the network tripled in weeks. CPUs were finished; the graphics card era had begun."]
	task.0EBB4060492440C3.title: "Out-hashed The First GPU Miners"
	quest.3E21E9F729ED3F8E.title: "Mining Records - The Network of 2010"
	quest.3E21E9F729ED3F8E.quest_desc: ["104 GH/s. Bitcoin ended 2010 at difficulty 14,484 - a thousandfold the year before. One bitcoin cost thirty cents."]
	task.358F429C21237BD5.title: "Out-hashed The Network of 2010"
	quest.615DED1D3E50BEE7.title: "Mining Records - The Network of 2011"
	quest.615DED1D3E50BEE7.quest_desc: ["8.3 TH/s. The year of the first bubble: one dollar in February, thirty in June, two by November. The network never noticed - it grew eightyfold."]
	task.450B852DC77F690E.title: "Out-hashed The Network of 2011"
	quest.3A2F95D9BA0FC5B9.title: "Mining Records - The First ASIC"
	quest.3A2F95D9BA0FC5B9.quest_desc: ["25 TH/s. In January 2013 Canaan's Avalon Batch 1 shipped - the first chip built for nothing but SHA-256. Within a year GPUs could no longer pay for their own electricity.", "Your first ASIC is in the Shop from the same date."]
	task.0B60E0F410C828E8.title: "Out-hashed The First ASIC"
	quest.00236C4D1066AAA0.title: "Mining Records - The Network of 2013"
	quest.00236C4D1066AAA0.quest_desc: ["6.5 PH/s. Difficulty rose 270-fold in one year as the ASICs arrived. Bitcoin touched $1,100 in December."]
	task.2ADEC7058DD7D1F1.title: "Out-hashed The Network of 2013"
	quest.6CD1E8E8473B9D57.title: "Mining Records - The Day Mt. Gox Fell"
	quest.6CD1E8E8473B9D57.quest_desc: ["25 PH/s. On 24 February 2014 the exchange that handled most of the world's bitcoin went dark, 850,000 BTC gone. The network kept hashing as if nothing had happened - because for the network, nothing had."]
	task.10274C7BD396E7E3.title: "Out-hashed The Day Mt. Gox Fell"
	quest.6ABA0848F976853B.title: "Mining Records - Fifty-One Percent"
	quest.6ABA0848F976853B.quest_desc: ["55 PH/s. On 13 June 2014 the GHash.io pool briefly controlled a majority of the network - enough to rewrite history, had it wanted to. Miners left it in a panic within days.", "You now hold what one pool once held over everyone."]
	task.2775591E20310A0B.title: "Out-hashed Fifty-One Percent"
	quest.3DD3507C06B3CB06.title: "Mining Records - The Network of 2014"
	quest.3DD3507C06B3CB06.quest_desc: ["286 PH/s. The price fell all year; the hashrate quadrupled anyway. Industrial mining had arrived: warehouses, not bedrooms."]
	task.5DD45A6C0D77A867.title: "Out-hashed The Network of 2014"
	quest.6E85CB2FC92EF340.title: "Mining Records - The Network of 2015"
	quest.6E85CB2FC92EF340.quest_desc: ["520 PH/s. Half an exahash. The 16 nm ASICs arrived and China's hydro dams became the cheapest hashpower on earth."]
	task.744E31994DBDB228.title: "Out-hashed The Network of 2015"
	quest.3AED5281E0DEBDBD.title: "Mining Records - The Second Halving"
	quest.3AED5281E0DEBDBD.quest_desc: ["1.5 EH/s. On 9 July 2016 the block reward fell from 25 to 12.5 BTC. Every miner's income halved overnight; the network shrugged."]
	task.47311F731F2F5175.title: "Out-hashed The Second Halving"
	quest.1E95438FF02391E4.title: "Mining Records - The Network of 2016"
	quest.1E95438FF02391E4.quest_desc: ["2.2 EH/s. Two exahashes - two billion billion hashes a second, every second, worldwide. Your island does that now."]
	task.5FF57FAFDBCC7225.title: "Out-hashed The Network of 2016"
	quest.5400382F87B51C4E.title: "Mining Records - The Network of 2017"
	quest.5400382F87B51C4E.quest_desc: ["13 EH/s. The year bitcoin hit $20,000 and the network passed ten exahashes. Past this point the real network runs away from anything one operator can build.", "Past here the ladder keeps climbing - but only in containers."]
	task.164D5AD08A8388B8.title: "Out-hashed The Network of 2017"
	quest.17778A85224E091B.title: "Contract - Containerized"
	quest.17778A85224E091B.quest_desc: ["Three hundred miners in three hundred blocks is a slideshow. A Mining Container takes them as items - a 2x2 box of shells behind the doors, 2 to 12 long, 27 units a slice, 324 in all - and runs them as one machine: one feed, one heat model, one water manifold.", "Buy one. The Heat Exchanger that comes with it is for what its hydro units will send you."]
	quest.224AB53F2DAF4AFE.title: "Mining Records - The Network of 2018"
	quest.224AB53F2DAF4AFE.quest_desc: ["37 EH/s. The year after the bubble: price down 80%, hashrate up threefold anyway. The 7 nm ASICs arrived and the first mining containers rolled out to the hydro dams.", "A hundred and thirty full hydro containers. This is where the container earns its keep."]
	task.2C541E213F565EE6.title: "Out-hashed The Network of 2018"
	quest.531B7225D7B85B37.title: "Mining Records - The Network of 2019"
	quest.531B7225D7B85B37.quest_desc: ["93 EH/s. Bitmain's S17 and MicroBT's M20 turned mining into an industry of megawatt campuses. Three hundred and thirty containers, 1.2 gigawatts."]
	task.078FCE7CEB57E03A.title: "Out-hashed The Network of 2019"
	quest.69F426BD9740FAC6.title: "Mining Records - The Network of 2020"
	quest.69F426BD9740FAC6.quest_desc: ["133 EH/s. The third halving cut the reward to 6.25 BTC in May; the network kept growing through it and through the pandemic. Nearly five hundred containers."]
	task.06599A435E983A2E.title: "Out-hashed The Network of 2020"
	quest.4226179B99FD1B0D.title: "Mining Records - The Network of 2021"
	quest.4226179B99FD1B0D.quest_desc: ["172 EH/s. In June China banned mining and half the world's hashrate went dark overnight - and had recovered by December, rebuilt in Texas and Kazakhstan. Six hundred containers."]
	task.23BCC6C4FC29B3B1.title: "Out-hashed The Network of 2021"
	quest.0817832A3AF50783.title: "Mining Records - The Network of 2022"
	quest.0817832A3AF50783.quest_desc: ["250 EH/s. Price down 65%, the big public miners in bankruptcy court - and the network still grew by half. Nine hundred containers, 3.2 gigawatts."]
	task.1755D53E3C56B002.title: "Out-hashed The Network of 2022"
	quest.1CAE4AB0F9171496.title: "Mining Records - The Network of 2023"
	quest.1CAE4AB0F9171496.quest_desc: ["515 EH/s. Half a zettahash. The S21 generation and immersion cooling doubled the network in a single year. Eighteen hundred containers, six and a half gigawatts."]
	task.080C0624506CEABC.title: "Out-hashed The Network of 2023"
	quest.61407C97FC5DBD67.title: "Mining Records - The Network of 2024"
	quest.61407C97FC5DBD67.quest_desc: ["770 EH/s. The fourth halving in April, bitcoin past $100,000 in December, and a network that would need nine hundred thousand hydro units, 2,800 full containers and ten gigawatts to match. Real size, real wall.", "Not the end: 2025 crossed a zettahash, and 2026 is the present."]
	task.37AD7BF58EEE0723.title: "Out-hashed The Network of 2024"
	quest.32070E2A4C056A24.title: "Hall of Fame - El Capitan"
	quest.32070E2A4C056A24.quest_desc: ["1.74 EFLOPS. Lawrence Livermore's El Capitan took #1 in November 2024 on 44,544 AMD MI300A - CPU and GPU on one chip - the fastest machine ever measured by HPL.", "Frontier's successor, built by the same people."]
	task.0D8ACFC906A32D57.title: "Out-computed El Capitan"
	quest.3AA0DDA9DF5B1891.title: "Hall of Fame - Colossus"
	quest.3AA0DDA9DF5B1891.quest_desc: ["~13 EFLOPS FP32. The Colossus campus in Memphis: 200,000 H100-class GPUs stood up in months, running on gas turbines because the grid could not follow - a quarter of a gigawatt for one training run.", "This is where the world went past supercomputers."]
	task.0619423F31A9B29D.title: "Out-computed the Colossus campus"
	quest.54A38954708AFD53.title: "Hall of Fame - Stargate"
	quest.54A38954708AFD53.quest_desc: ["~32 EFLOPS FP32. The Stargate campus in Abilene, 2026: roughly 400,000 GB200 GPUs and 1.2 gigawatts of power, the first phase of the largest computer ever attempted.", "The end of the ladder in 2026. Whatever you built to get here, it is bigger than anything on Earth today."]
	task.3FB8C10474641B7B.title: "Out-computed the Stargate campus"
	quest.0A4A8A967376F96A.title: "Mining Records - One Zettahash"
	quest.0A4A8A967376F96A.quest_desc: ["1,000 EH/s. In 2025 the Bitcoin network crossed a zettahash - a thousand billion billion hashes a second - and kept going. Real size: over a million of the best hydro units and twelve gigawatts."]
	task.3B3405810DD6876F.title: "Out-hashed the network of 2025"
	quest.2E57D8CA4CD04E16.title: "Mining Records - The Network Today"
	quest.2E57D8CA4CD04E16.quest_desc: ["1,200 EH/s. The network as it stood in mid-2026, when this ladder was written. The Antminer S23 Hyd 3U, the first 2026 unit, does 1.16 PH/s at 11 kW - you would need a million of them.", "The top of the ladder is the present. From here on, history is yours to write."]
	task.60462E1D6CB76961.title: "Out-hashed the network of mid-2026"
	quest.3B76B3B36CB812BE.title: "Contract - On the Grid"
	quest.3B76B3B36CB812BE.quest_desc: ["Real data centers buy their power. A Grid Connection feeds every machine touching it and every cable that pulls from it, without limit, and bills your Wallet for every kWh at the real US industrial rate of the day - about 7 cents in 2009.", "Own generation is the way around the bill. Right-click the cabinet for its meter panel and set the draw limit to what your Wallet can carry."]
	task.11A1541681633810.title: "Own a Grid Connection"
	quest.11921F8D3E041B33.title: "Acceptance Test - First Bill"
	quest.11921F8D3E041B33.quest_desc: ["Draw power from the grid and let the meter run until the first cent is debited. One FE per tick is one watt; a 2 kW rack costs about $1.70 a real minute.", "No money, no power: an unpaid bill cuts the feed until the arrears are covered."]
	task.0CEF27D2737E7235.title: "Paid an electricity bill"
	quest.16C937A69A0EE81F.title: "Incident - Dead on Arrival"
	quest.16C937A69A0EE81F.quest_desc: ["Parts do not only wear out, they die: about 2% of drives and PSUs a year, 1.5% of graphics cards, all the faster the hotter they run. When one goes, chat names the part, the machine and the bay.", "Pull it at the Workbench; the Salvage Bench refurbishes or strips it like any worn-out part."]
	task.238747326F32B526.title: "A part failed at random"
	quest.7FED25E94AB49FCB.title: "Acceptance Test - RMA"
	quest.7FED25E94AB49FCB.quest_desc: ["Parts bought in the Shop carry their real warranty: three years on processors and cards, seven on power supplies, ten on memory, two on fans, thirty days second-hand. A random failure inside the term is replaced free on the spot.", "Hover a part to see until when it is covered. Wear is never a warranty case."]
	task.5A1BC1BCB0F6C2D9.title: "A failed part was replaced under warranty"
	quest.024D5CD7F47F2DEB.title: "Lucky Strike"
	quest.024D5CD7F47F2DEB.quest_desc: ["Before 27 November 2010 there were no pools: you found a whole block or nothing. Afterwards the button in the Mining Rig panel switches your world between a pool with its 2% fee and solo mining.", "Find one block solo - fifty BTC and the day's fees in one go."]
	task.3EA70C525F6B5819.title: "Found a block mining solo"
}
